#{[17592186305093 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305093 #edb/ref 1069 "q-332678" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305093 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305093 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305093 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "14b93aa5-65d3-5ddc-9724-ab937ea4f1a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305093 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-5t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n\\\n\n![](images/3f/3facfdf59a92a9202d834cd77d14e009c4ec69c5f1b7bd5f12bd17ea1bbc338b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305099 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305099 #edb/ref 1069 "q-332705" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305099 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305099 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/98/98ffa8fec669ce09d22b5b904b710632314f7b22e0eb5098719c41147c186975.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305099 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f20c0d44-466d-592f-adfd-5535d1164b77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305099 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust also approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}>0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305100 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305100 #edb/ref 1069 "q-332707" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305100 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305100 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/52/5243b22ebef45709f214ac2422cfb72528f24fd5ef51bd38aa3fc0ff40f1888d.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305100 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1e70744b-cd4d-5289-8a1e-db4fcf31bc45" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305100 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust also approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}>0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305102 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305102 #edb/ref 1069 "q-332713" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305102 #edb/r