f 17592186306545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307683 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "337c1171-efd2-5892-8314-2112fd9080ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307929 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307929 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307929 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186306904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307929 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8230b11d-8bb8-5c4c-873b-8b24da7d6f03" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308020 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308020 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308020 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308020 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308020 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186306064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308020 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9e4bd274-b5ff-536c-adca-f46c1bdd94e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308404 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308404 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333184" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308404 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308404 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308404 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bf46b344-09be-5328-8704-8fe72b540c14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308404 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{6t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/24/2473117cdca4318187b94374820c0a12576e998e7f631a6e7bf51e56c985be43.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{6t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{4t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{6t})^{′}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}}{6{c}_{1}{e}^{6t}}=\\frac{2{c}_{2}}{3{c}_{1}}{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/e8/e8110ea6d6a113e72e35502030afb495a23bc81106d64874582d03aae81b7c73.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308410 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308410 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308410 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308410 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333214" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308410 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308410 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308410 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "59947953-f704-5cbc-beaf-56322b9434f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308410 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/6d/6dc8ceb9a1c771f18d6e90d450cd837fe80c6bab7943ab7d397d90eb45754594.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391043 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391043 #edb/ref 1069 "q-332683" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391043 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391043 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-7y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391043 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3f7b6c7b-9bbd-58e1-9f4f-ab6d8faeb7f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391043 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-7t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-7t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-7t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/d6/d604781deed3206caf0a3bf6afa857c00b